{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed distinguishing evidence—intervening on one node's failure rate and checking for changes in distant nodes' rates—is a causal-sufficiency test for independence, but it is not the right test for the problem's core concern: failures that 'merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found.' Hidden common-cause failures (e.g., a shared DNS provider or shared time-sync protocol, which SRC_001 itself names in its counterconditions) are not caused by one node's failure propagating to distant nodes; they are jointly triggered by an exogenous shared event. Under the do-operator on node A's failure, the shared cause is held fixed, so the distant nodes' marginal failure rates need not change even though their failures are strongly correlated under the shared trigger. The test would declare them independent when they are not. The mechanism never supplies a causal pathway from the proposed intervention to the latent shared cause that actually drives correlated failure, so it fails to distinguish the two failure modes the problem asks about.","counterexample":["Two independent-looking nodes A and B both depend on a shared DNS provider. Under do(fail A), B's failure rate is unchanged; the proposed test classifies them as independent. But when the DNS provider fails, both fail simultaneously—the dependence was hidden, not absent."],"target":"0e816fc8dad5ae6b876e59e37d0e817495a78fdfcc92db0a66598e3b92c50d67"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 proposes testing the stability of the correlation structure under environmental perturbation as the distinguishing evidence. But the causal mechanism is underspecified and partly circular: to identify a correlation structure as 'stable under perturbation,' one must already know which perturbations are legitimate environmental changes versus which are the shared causes themselves. If the 'shared stable environment' whose change reveals hidden dependence is the very kind of common dependency that causes correlated failure (e.g., shared cloud provider), then the test reduces to 'if the shared cause fails, failures correlate'—which is the phenomenon to be detected, not a discriminating mechanism. SRC_002 never supplies a causal criterion that separates a perturbation that merely stresses the same shared cause from one that genuinely tests independence, so the evidence criterion cannot do the distinguishing work the problem demands.","counterexample":["A decentralized network runs on two cloud providers that appear independent in the baseline correlation structure. Perturbing by forcing a switch to a third, less-common provider increases observed correlation because the third provider is a shared bottleneck; the test now says independence was merely apparent, yet the original two-provider setup was genuinely independent with respect to the original failure modes. Without a prior causal model, the same perturbation outcome is consistent with both true independence and hidden dependence."],"target":"193032467bde156621d9aeb9180587491c3540c92294ddfbd1fa6732e96d819d"}]}